  "step_2": "The individual considered the possible monetary gain and the probability of winning. Specifically, they noted that Lottery B offers a higher possible gain (4000 Euros vs. 3000 Euros), but the probability of winning is only slightly lower (20% vs. 25%). The individual highlights the trade-off between the higher potential reward and the small reduction in probability. The justification centers on the comparison of the maximum payout and the near-identical probability of winning, suggesting a focus on the magnitude of the gain relative to the slight drop in chance. No mention is made of the distribution of probability mass across outcomes, nor is there any reference to the concept of skewness or the relative weight of favorable outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating which lottery assigns a greater proportion of probability to favorable outcomes (i.e., left-skewness). The individual did not consider the proportion of probability on favorable outcomes; instead, they focused on the absolute size of the maximum payoff and the small difference in winning probabilities. The individual’s reasoning is based on the trade-off between higher reward and slightly lower chance, which aligns more with expected value or risk-return trade-offs, not skewness. There is no mention of probability distribution shape or the relative weight of favorable outcomes. Therefore, the reasoning process described does not match the rule of the decision reason. The individual did not use the skewness-based criterion to make the decision.",
